  <article class="prose prose-slate max-w-none mb-12">
    <h2>1. Classical Pedestal Origins: The Vitruvian Triad</h2>
    <p>
      In classical Greco-Roman architecture, a column rests upon a three-part pedestal: the base (plinth), the body (dado), and the cap (surbase). Interior wainscoting translates this exact tripartite order to residential interior walls:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Baseboard:</strong> Represents the architectural plinth, grounding the wall to the floor.</li>
      <li><strong>Wainscot Panels (Picture Frames or Beadboard):</strong> Represents the dado body, occupying the lower third of the vertical wall space.</li>
      <li><strong>Chair Rail:</strong> Represents the surbase cap, terminating the lower architectural element and protecting the plaster above.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Under classical rules, the pedestal occupies approximately <strong>one-third (33.3%)</strong> of the total vertical height. When interior designers break this rule by placing the chair rail at 50% (e.g. 48 inches on an 8-foot wall), the eye perceives two equal halves, eliminating vertical tension and visually cutting the room in half.
    </p>

    <h2>2. Ceiling Height Master Rules: 8-Foot, 9-Foot, and 10-Foot Walls</h2>
    <p>
      Use these proven architectural benchmarks based on finished floor-to-ceiling measurements:
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6">
      <table class="w-full text-sm text-left border border-slate-200 rounded-lg">
        <thead class="bg-slate-100 text-slate-800 font-bold border-b border-slate-200">
          <tr>
            <th class="py-3 px-4">Ceiling Height</th>
            <th class="py-3 px-4">Exact 1/3 Ratio</th>
            <th class="py-3 px-4">Standard Practical Range</th>
            <th class="py-3 px-4">High 2/3 (Tall B&amp;B)</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200 text-slate-700">
          <tr>
            <td class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold text-slate-900">8 Feet (96 inches)</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4 font-mono text-sky-700">32.0 inches</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4 font-bold">32 to 34 inches</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4">58 to 64 inches</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold text-slate-900">9 Feet (108 inches)</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4 font-mono text-sky-700">36.0 inches</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4 font-bold">35 to 38 inches</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4">66 to 72 inches</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold text-slate-900">10 Feet (120 inches)</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4 font-mono text-sky-700">40.0 inches</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4 font-bold">38 to 42 inches</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4">76 to 82 inches</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>3. Navigating Window Sills &amp; Architectural Obstacles</h2>
    <p>
      The most common design dilemma occurs when the calculated 1/3 chair rail height lands 1 or 2 inches above or below an existing window sill apron:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>The Window Stool Integration Rule:</strong> If your calculated 1/3 rail height lands within 2 inches of the bottom of the window stool (the projecting wooden sill ledge), adjust your chair rail elevation to run continuously directly into or directly below the window stool. This creates a unified architectural sill band instead of an awkward 1-inch slice of painted drywall between the rail and the window.</li>
      <li><strong>Radiator &amp; Baseboard Heater Clearances:</strong> If hot water baseboards or cast iron radiators occupy lower walls, ensure the bottom rail of your picture frames clears the heating unit by a minimum of 3 inches to avoid heat-induced miter separation and paint peeling.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>4. The Modern Alternative: Two-Thirds (2/3) High Wainscoting</h2>
    <p>
      While formal dining rooms and traditional spaces favor the 1/3 rule, contemporary Craftsman, modern farmhouse, and coastal entryways frequently utilize the <strong>two-thirds (2/3) rule</strong>:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>For an 8-foot ceiling, the top horizontal cap rail is placed at <strong>60 to 64 inches</strong> above the floor.</li>
      <li>This creates a striking, enveloping effect in foyers, powder rooms, and master bedrooms, providing an ideal height for coat hooks, plate rails, or bold wallpaper above the paneled lower section.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <!-- Trade Pro Field Note -->
  <div class="mb-10">
    <FieldNote
      title="The Staircase Rake Transition Rule"
      trade="Architectural Trim"
      content="Transitioning a horizontal dining room chair rail onto an ascending staircase requires precise geometric planning. Never angle the chair rail abruptly at the first stair tread. Instead, project the level chair rail 6 inches past the first riser, establish the plumb line of the newel post, and transition into the staircase rake angle at an elevation that matches the handrail height (typically 36 inches above the stair tread nosing per IRC R311.7.8)."
    />
  </div>

  <!-- Critical Costly Mistakes Box -->
  <div class="mb-12">
    <CostlyMistakes
      projectType="Wainscoting Design"
      mistakes={[
        {
          mistake: "Letting Chair Rail Collide with Light Switches",
          impact: "A chair rail installed at 38 inches often slices directly through standard 40-inch light switch wall plates, forcing bizarre jigsaw cutouts in the moulding.",
          prevention: "Check switch box heights first. Standard residential switches are mounted at 42 inches to center; ensure your chair rail stays at 34 to 36 inches."
        },
        {
          mistake: "Ignoring Baseboard Height When Sizing Picture Frames",
          impact: "If you install a massive 7-inch speed baseboard and a 3-inch chair rail, an arbitrary 36-inch height leaves only a squat 24-inch vertical frame space.",
          prevention: "Calculate the net panel field: subtract baseboard height, chair rail width, and dual margin reveals from total rail height before finalizing box proportions."
        }
      ]}
    />
